<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mikhail Bakunin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1814">1814</a>–<a href="/years/1876">1876</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Mikhail Alexandrovich Bakunin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mikhail Bakunin was a Russian revolutionary anarchist and political philosopher, among the most influential figures of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anarchism">anarchism</a>. Born into a noble family at Pryamukhino in the Tver <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a>, he took part in the 1848 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> and 1849 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> uprisings, was sentenced to death and extradited several times, and escaped from exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> via <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. In 1868 he joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-workingmens-association">International Workingmen's Association</a>, where he led the anarchist faction against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a> until his expulsion in 1872. He argued that Marxist states would be one-party <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorships</a> ruling over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/proletariat">proletariat</a>, and his book God and the State remains in print.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mikhail-bakunin.jpg" alt="Mikhail Bakunin" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nadar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mikhail_Bakunin_Nader_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mikhail Alexandrovich Bakunin was born on 30 May 1814 (18 May in the old style) into the Russian nobility. His family's Priamukhino estate in the Tver region had over 500 serfs. His father, Alexander Mikhailovich Bakunin, a diplomat who had served in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, raised ten children on the Rousseauan model, and Mikhail was the third child and eldest son.</p><p>He trained as a military officer, deserted in 1835 and was discharged. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> he joined the circle of Nikolai Stankevich, read German philosophy and produced the first Russian translation of Hegel. Alexander Herzen funded his studies at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humboldt-university-of-berlin">University of Berlin</a> from 1840, where he drew towards the Young Hegelians. His first original publication, The Reaction in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, appeared in 1842 and marked his shift from philosophy to revolutionary rhetoric.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Bakunin left for Zürich in 1843, and when he defied Russian orders to return, the Russian Senate stripped him of his noble rights and sentenced him in absentia to penal labour in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> he befriended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-joseph-proudhon">Pierre-Joseph Proudhon</a> and met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a>. A speech to Polish emigrants in 1847 led to his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. He took part in the Prague Slavic Congress and its insurrection in 1848, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> uprising of 1849.</p><p>Imprisoned, sentenced to death and extradited several times, he was held in solitary confinement in the Peter and Paul Fortress in 1851. Exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> in 1857, he escaped in 1861 via <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, where he worked with Herzen on the journal Kolokol.</p><p>He joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-workingmens-association">International Workingmen's Association</a> in 1868. At the 1872 Hague Congress, which he could not reach, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a>'s side prevailed and Bakunin was expelled, after which he founded the Anti-Authoritarian International. He took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> insurrection of 1870, wrote Statism and Anarchy in 1873, and planned an unsuccessful insurrection at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> in 1874. He died in Bern on 1 July 1876.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Bakunin wrote prolifically but fragmentarily, and was not a systematic thinker; much of his work was published after his death. He first called himself an anarchist in 1867. He wished to replace the state and hierarchical religious, social and economic institutions with free federations of communes and voluntary associations of producers, organised from below upward. God and the State, published in 1882 from a fragment, became his most widely translated work.</p><p>He rejected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a>'s idea of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/proletariat">proletariat</a>, warning that a Marxist state would rule over the workers rather than be ruled by them. His split with Marx divided the socialist movement from the anarchist one.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mikhail Bakunin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The International Workingmen’s Association, the First International, was founded at St Martin’s Hall in London on 28 September 1864; divided between the followers of Marx and the anarchists around <b>Mikhail Bakunin</b>, it split in 1872 and was dissolved in 1876.</blockquote><a href="/movement/labour-movement">Labour movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Many anarchists see the state as a tool of domination that takes decisions for a small elite and corrupts even altruistic officials; <b>Bakunin</b> defined it as “coercion, domination by means of coercion”.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/anarchism">Anarchism · Core Ideas</a></li><li><blockquote>Anarchists such as <b>Mikhail Bakunin</b> criticised Marx’s authoritarian bent, and libertarian and classical liberal economists argue that the lack of free markets leads to authoritarianism.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/marxism">Marxism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote>Its leader <b>Mikhail Bakunin</b> came to believe that worker organisations might need to use force; he and his followers advocated the general strike, rejected electoral politics and anticipated workers' organisations replacing rule by the state.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/syndicalism">Syndicalism · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Priamukhino, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in Bern, in what is now <a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=56.94,34.45&amp;year=1854" aria-label="Priamukhino on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5293 231 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mikhail Bakunin"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5743 501h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5743 501h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=56.94,34.45&amp;year=1854">What was here in 1854? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;A jealous lover of human liberty, and deeming it the absolute condition of all that we admire and respect in humanity, I reverse the phrase of Voltaire, and say that, if God really existed, it would be necessary to abolish him.&#8221;</p><footer>God and the State (written 1871) &#183; tr. Benjamin R. Tucker, 1883 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/36568/pg36568-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;In the matter of boots, I refer to the authority of the bootmaker; concerning houses, canals, or railroads, I consult that of the architect or engineer.&#8221;</p><footer>God and the State (written 1871) &#183; tr. Benjamin R. Tucker, 1883 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/36568/pg36568-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The liberty of man consists solely in this: that he obeys natural laws because he has himself recognized them as such, and not because they have been externally imposed upon him by any extrinsic will whatever, divine or human, collective or individual.&#8221;</p><footer>God and the State (written 1871) &#183; tr. Benjamin R. Tucker, 1883 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/36568/pg36568-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The idea of God implies the abdication of human reason and justice; it is the most decisive negation of human liberty, and necessarily ends in the enslavement of mankind, both in theory and practice.&#8221;</p><footer>God and the State (written 1871) &#183; tr. Benjamin R. Tucker, 1883 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/36568/pg36568-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I receive and I give—such is human life. Each directs and is directed in his turn. Therefore there is no fixed and constant authority, but a continual exchange of mutual, temporary, and, above all, voluntary authority and subordination.&#8221;</p><footer>God and the State (written 1871) &#183; tr. Benjamin R. Tucker, 1883 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/36568/pg36568-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
